    "intro": "A gutter estimate has one job: tell the homeowner exactly what you will do and what it costs, clearly enough that they can say yes without calling you back. Most estimates fail at that. They are a single number in a text message, or a page of fine print with no mention of downspouts, or a quote that arrives four days after the customer asked. This post covers how to gather what you need, how to lay out the estimate, which options to include, and how to get it out fast enough to matter. Pricing itself (what to charge per foot, height surcharges) has its own posts; this one is about the estimate as a document and a process.",
